    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Done. All tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 2/2 acceptance tests green, including the new structured-address checkpoint).\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe design centres on one idea: **compute the effective, normalized address once and store it in the flat `address` field**, so every downstream step reads a single canonical value regardless of which form the request used.\n\n**Contract (`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`)**\n- Added `addressLine1` (optional, 1\u2013255) and `addressLine2` (optional, \u2264255) to `OwnerFields` \u2014 so they appear in both the request DTO and the response (`Owner` extends `OwnerFields`).\n- Removed `address` from the `required` list (structured requests omit it), keeping it accepted for backward compatibility. Documented `address` as the returned composed value.\n\n**Domain (`Owner.java` + all four DB schemas)**\n- Added `addressLine1` / `addressLine2` columns and accessors so the structured fields persist and read back via GET. Seed inserts use explicit column lists, so they're unaffected.\n\n**Address logic (`AddressNormalizer.compose`)**\n- New reusable method encapsulating the rule: prefer `addressLine1` (normalized) with normalized `addressLine2` appended after a single space when present; otherwise fall back to the normalized flat `address`; empty string when neither is supplied. Reuses the existing `normalize`.\n\n**Wiring**\n- `NormalizeOwnerAddress` now normalizes whichever fields are supplied and sets `address` to the composed value.\n- `ValidateRequiredOwnerFields` checks the composed address, so a request is valid with a non-blank `addressLine1` **or** a flat `address` (plus city) \u2014 earlier minimal owners still accepted.\n- For consistency, PUT now runs the same `NormalizeOwnerAddress` step and `ApplyOwner` copies the structured fields \u2014 so updates behave identically to creates.\n\nBecause `city`/`postcode` are shared top-level fields and the composed value is stored in `address`, the household hash (lastName + postcode), postcode validation and locality all naturally read the effective address without further change.",
